  "equal_send_rate_attribution": {
    "rule": "br-kp1in.40 attribution (written 2026-10-13 ~13:30Z, after kp140d run 1 and before any run of this experiment).\n\nObservation that prompts it: in the closed-loop release-smoke soak, v0.3.39 (9404dba0) sends ~5x more than shipped\nv0.3.38 (171713fa) and serves ~0.31x the reads at ~6.8x the fetch p99 (run 1). Every soak send goes to reader B, so the\nfaster build grows B's inbox ~5x faster, and the inbox bench shows fetch_inbox cost grows with inbox size. The soak\ncannot tell \"reads got slower\" from \"the inbox got bigger faster\".\n\nExperiment: mixed_rate.py, soak-shaped (2 senders A->B, 2 closed-loop readers fetch_inbox(B, limit 20,\nmark_read false), 1 search loop, 1 Hz health), but the SENDERS ARE PACED at 4 sends/s total (open loop), identical for\nboth builds, so B's inbox grows identically. 300 s per run. Arms A = shipped v0.3.38 (171713fa), B = v0.3.39 candidate\n(9404dba0). Order: A B B A. Host lock; nothing else of mine on the host.\n\nValidity: every arm completes >= 95% of its scheduled sends (1,140 of 1,200), else the run is invalid (a build that\ncannot sustain 4/s is not at equal inbox size).\n\nDecision:\n  READS NOT SLOWER (the soak drop is the closed-loop confound): pooled B read count >= 0.90 x A and pooled B read p99\n    <= 1.5 x A. Then the bead's soak criterion measures send-rate-driven inbox growth; report that, keep the O(inbox)\n    fetch cost as its own issue (br-rkct2 for the shared read; the direct read gets a bead if its per-window latency\n    grows with sends_before).\n  READS SLOWER: either half fails. Then attribute inside the read (evidence-ledger phases) before any code change.\nPer-window read p50 vs sends_before is reported for both arms (cost vs inbox size).\n",
    "harness": "mixed_rate.py (scratchpad): soak shape, senders paced at 4/s total, 2 closed-loop readers fetch_inbox(B, limit 20, mark_read false), 1 search loop, 1 Hz health, 300 s",
